---
import SiteLayout from '../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const siteUrl = 'https://shadowharness.github.io/';
const pageUrl = `${siteUrl}en/security/`;
const title = 'Security & Trust — Shadow';
const description =
  "How Shadow handles your conversation data — what we store, how it's isolated, encryption, and your right to erase or export it, explained plainly.";
---

<SiteLayout
	lang="en"
	title={title}
	description={description}
	pageUrl={pageUrl}
	alternateJa={`${siteUrl}security/`}
	alternateEn={pageUrl}
>
		<main>
			<section class="section page-lead">
				<p class="eyebrow"><a href={`${base}en/`}>← Back to SHADOW</a></p>
				<h1>Security &amp; Trust</h1>
				<p class="lead">
					A conversation log with an AI almost certainly contains personal information and
					the substance of your work. Shadow is designed on that assumption. Here's a plain,
					unhidden account of how Shadow stores your data and protects it.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section id="what-we-hold" class="section alt">
				<h2>What we store — and don't</h2>
				<p class="lead">
					What Shadow stores is your conversation records with AI, and the memory notes
					(summaries and indexes) built from them automatically. All of it is your asset —
					Shadow keeps it so an AI can recall what you did last time, the next time you sit
					down to work.
				</p>
				<div class="grid-2">
					<div class="card">
						<h3>What we store</h3>
						<p>Conversation records, and the memory notes built from them. All of it belongs to you.</p>
					</div>
					<div class="card">
						<h3>What we don't store</h3>
						<p>
							Files on your machine, passwords, API keys, work output. None of that ever
							leaves your PC — only conversation records are sent to the server.
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>
				<p class="lead">
					The original conversation record always stays on your machine too. The server
					holds a copy to process memory from — it is not a vault that holds your data
					hostage.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section id="isolation" class="section">
				<h2>One tenant, one room — isolation by design, not by permissions</h2>
				<p class="lead">
					Your data lives in a room (a container) isolated just for you. It is never placed
					alongside another user's data.
				</p>
				<p class="lead">
					Most services put everyone's data in one shared warehouse and separate it with
					access permissions. That design means a single permission bug can expose someone
					else's data. Shadow built the opposite: inside your room, another user's data simply
					does not exist. Even if a flaw showed up in your room's own software, the only thing
					reachable from it is your own data.
				</p>
				<p class="lead">
					In terms of how far the separation goes: Shadow sits between "one shared database
					for everyone" and "one physical server per user." The host OS, the container
					platform, and the network entry point are shared, but the place where conversation
					data is stored and the program that processes memory are split into a dedicated
					container and a dedicated data volume, one per user.
				</p>
				<p class="lead">
					That structural choice contains the kind of accident a shared database can produce
					— a mix-up that exposes another user's data — to a single room, without the cost of
					a dedicated physical server per user.
				</p>
				<p class="lead">
					Each room also has its own key. There is no master key for every room sitting at
					the server's front door. If the front door is ever breached, the keys to other
					rooms are not part of the loot.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section id="operators" class="section alt">
				<h2>What operators can see</h2>
				<p class="lead">
					Whoever operates the server is, technically, in a position to reach the data inside
					it. That's true of any cloud service.
				</p>
				<div class="grid-3">
					<div class="card">
						<h3>Only for your request or a real incident</h3>
						<p>Operators open your data only when you ask us to investigate an issue, or when it's necessary to recover from a system failure.</p>
					</div>
					<div class="card">
						<h3>Every access is logged</h3>
						<p>Whenever data is opened, when and why is recorded.</p>
					</div>
					<div class="card">
						<h3>Never for sales or advertising</h3>
						<p>Never used for sales or advertising. Never sold or handed to a third party.</p>
					</div>
				</div>
			</section>

			<section id="ai-summary" class="section">
				<h2>AI summarization</h2>
				<p class="lead">
					The conversation records we hold are handed to a dedicated AI to turn into memory
					notes. Two commitments here:
				</p>
				<div class="grid-2">
					<div class="card">
						<h3>Never used for training</h3>
						<p>The AI we use for summarization runs under terms where input is not used to train the model.</p>
					</div>
					<div class="card">
						<h3>We cover the cost — we don't hold your key</h3>
						<p>
							Holding your API key would put the risk of a leak, and the fine print of
							your key's own contract, on your shoulders. We don't take it.
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>
			</section>

			<section id="transport" class="section alt">
				<h2>Transport and keys</h2>
				<div class="grid-2">
					<div class="card">
						<h3>Encrypted in transit</h3>
						<p>Everything between your machine and the server travels over an encrypted channel. Your records never cross the network in the clear.</p>
					</div>
					<div class="card">
						<h3>A separate key per device</h3>
						<p>Each device uses its own key (token). Lose a phone, and only that device's key gets revoked — instantly.</p>
					</div>
					<div class="card">
						<h3>Only a fingerprint is stored</h3>
						<p>The server keeps a hash for verification, never the key itself — there is nothing to steal from the server that would work as a key.</p>
					</div>
					<div class="card">
						<h3>The admin path is fully separate</h3>
						<p>The server's administrative route is completely separate from the one you use. Nothing reachable from your side of the door can reach admin operations.</p>
					</div>
				</div>
			</section>

			<section id="retention" class="section">
				<h2>Never quietly deleted, always deletable</h2>
				<div class="grid-3">
					<div class="card">
						<h3>Never quietly deleted</h3>
						<p>
							There is no code path on the server that truncates or overwrites a stored
							conversation record. That guards against a bug or a mistake eating your
							history, simply by not building the capability. Nightly backups are also
							kept as separate generations elsewhere.
						</p>
					</div>
					<div class="card">
						<h3>You can delete it</h3>
						<p>Close your account and your room is deleted entirely, backups included within the retention window (exact days will be spelled out in the Terms of Service).</p>
					</div>
					<div class="card">
						<h3>You can take it with you</h3>
						<p>Memory notes and the summarized conversation range can be exported anytime. The complete original conversation always lives on your machine anyway.</p>
					</div>
				</div>
			</section>

			<section id="dont-have" class="section alt">
				<h2>What we don't have</h2>
				<p class="lead">
					The strongest protection is simply not holding something in the first place.
					Here's what Shadow doesn't have:
				</p>
				<div class="grid-3">
					<div class="card">
						<h3>Your password</h3>
						<p>It doesn't exist here. You sign in through an external account (Google, etc.), and the password stays between you and that company — it never even touches our server.</p>
					</div>
					<div class="card">
						<h3>Your name or profile</h3>
						<p>Not stored. All we get from the external account is an identifier tied to it, with no name attached — enough to know "same person as last time," nothing more.</p>
					</div>
					<div class="card">
						<h3>Your card details</h3>
						<p>Those go straight to the payment processor (Stripe); our server never sees them.</p>
					</div>
				</div>
				<p class="lead">
					What we do hold: your conversation data (what we're storing on your behalf), a
					nameless identifier tied to your external account, one contact email address, and
					a list of the devices you've connected (display name and the date you added it —
					the minimum needed to let you cut off just one lost device). The email is used only
					for incident notices and important announcements — never marketing.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section id="wont-do" class="section">
				<h2>What we will never do</h2>
				<div class="grid-2">
					<div class="card">
						<h3>Sell, advertise with, or train on your data</h3>
						<p>We don't sell your data, use it for ads, or use it to train any AI.</p>
					</div>
					<div class="card">
						<h3>Reach into your machine uninvited</h3>
						<p>Every connection starts from your PC. There is no path from our server back to your PC.</p>
					</div>
					<div class="card">
						<h3>Build a master key</h3>
						<p>No master key exists that works across every user.</p>
					</div>
					<div class="card">
						<h3>Leak internal information</h3>
						<p>We don't return internal server information in a response — including anything that would let you infer another user exists.</p>
					</div>
				</div>
			</section>

			<section id="incident" class="section alt">
				<h2>Our promise when something goes wrong</h2>
				<p class="lead">
					If something happens that could affect your data — a suspected intrusion, data
					loss, a serious outage — and we become aware of it, we tell the affected user,
					without hiding it and without delay, what happened and what we fixed.
				</p>
			</section>
		</main>
</SiteLayout>

<style>
	.page-lead {
		padding-top: clamp(3rem, 10vh, 5rem);
		padding-bottom: 1rem;
	}
	.page-lead .eyebrow {
		margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
	}
	.page-lead .eyebrow a {
		text-decoration: none;
		font-size: 0.85rem;
		font-weight: 700;
		letter-spacing: 0.03em;
		color: #9db0c3;
		transition: color 0.15s ease;
	}
	.page-lead .eyebrow a:hover {
		color: #38dbe6;
	}
	.page-lead h1 {
		font-size: clamp(1.9rem, 5vw, 2.6rem);
		margin: 0 0 1.1rem;
		color: #f4fbfd;
	}
	#isolation .lead,
	#operators .lead,
	#ai-summary .lead,
	#dont-have .lead {
		max-width: 760px;
	}
</style>
